# Optional dashboard age declaration

This generated v13 preview asks for an explicit declaration before each dashboard
visit. It does not verify age, capture a photo, request a birth date or establish
guardianship. Use it only when the service's assessed policy permits declarations.
Other actions, including billing, retain their own authorization requirements.

Both dashboard routes run the existing session/user lookup and CSRF middleware.
GET presents the server-owned threshold; POST consumes the challenge before
rendering the existing dashboard. No reusable account flag, cookie or bearer
permission is created. A denied, missing, expired, altered or already-used
declaration never reveals the dashboard. Storage uncertainty may consume a
challenge; retry with a fresh one. The one-use age claim and application action
are not a distributed transaction.

Configure these server-only variables through the deployment secret manager:

- `RULLST_AGE_KEY_HEX`: an independent random 32-byte key encoded as 64 hex
  characters. Do not reuse APP_KEY, commit it or send it to the browser.
- `RULLST_AGE_KEY_ID`: a non-secret key epoch. Rotate key and ID together on all
  instances; this scaffold invalidates outstanding challenges during rotation.
- `RULLST_AGE_REPLAY_DATABASE`: the private SQLite file path or PostgreSQL
  connection URL matching the generated `__PROFILE__` profile. All instances
  share the same authoritative store and fixed 10,000-live-claim capacity.

Initialize PostgreSQL once in a deployment step with
`PostgresReplayStore::initialize(url, 10_000)`; application startup uses `connect`
and does not create/repair state. SQLite creates its file in an existing private
directory and is supported only on one host, never on a network filesystem.
Remote PostgreSQL verifies TLS; replication/failover, clocks, permissions,
backups and durable hardware remain operator responsibilities. A restored old
database requires quiescing verification and rotating the policy/key epoch.

The policy version, minimum age and single application tenant reference live in
`src/controllers/age_gate/config.rs`. Resolve actual tenant membership from
authoritative application state before adapting this single-tenant starter to
a multi-tenant service. Do not accept tenant, user or policy values from a form.
The challenge contains keyed opaque references, is authenticated but not
encrypted, and must stay out of URLs/logs/analytics. TLS, request limits and the
Server security baseline remain mandatory. Responses are marked `no-store`.

This preview uses an explicitly selected unpublished local privacy package.
Review and remove that source path only after the corresponding v13 package is
admitted and published. There is no automatic global-law compliance claim or
implemented stronger-verification fallback in this generated journey.
